{"step_index":2433,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T04:29:10Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nLets retry this and run well and hard. monitor the mac so we do not loose power or connection again. lets stop the revering to py, I keep getting eror message for it. and we do not have enough of our own code in use and I am sure we did basic work even on it. and we can take it over the top to master all and be the best for all users.  \"lets show true numbers and lets get real data. Those numbers are physically impossible, and that's the first thing I'd flag before anyone acts on this audit.\n\n11 TB/s of unified memory bandwidth on an M4 is roughly 5× the chip's actual peak — real M4 bandwidth sits around 120 GB/s for LPDDR5X. The GFLOPS figures are derived from the same wall-clock, so they're inflated by the same factor. The 0.00 µs p50 latency is a quantization artifact: your timer resolution can't resolve sub-microsecond events, so it's reporting zero instead of \"below the noise floor.\"\n\nThe relative ordering is probably real — C++ and SOVRA C edging Rust and Zig, Go trailing by ~150× is consistent with Go's lack of auto-vectorization on tight index loops. But the absolute scale is fiction, and a 503 from the Gemini backend mid-run means some of those rows may have been filled by a retry path rather than a clean measurement.\n\nBefore the council locks Rust-plus-C++ as the production split, re-run with a calibrated timer — mach_absolute_time or std::chrono::steady_clock with a warm-up pass and at least 50 iterations — and pin the actual bandwidth with a streaming copy benchmark so you have a ground-truth denominator. The ranking survives; the magnitudes don't.\"\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-29T00:29:10-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
